From: Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: qcom-legacy: restore the QSCRATCH settings on
 resume

The legacy glue sets the VBUS override (QSCRATCH_HS_PHY_CTRL's
UTMI_OTG_VBUS_VALID and SW_SESSVLD_SEL, QSCRATCH_SS_PHY_CTRL's
LANE0_PWR_PRESENT) and, with qcom,select-utmi-as-pipe-clk, the UTMI clock
as pipe clock once at probe, and again only from its extcon notifiers.
On SM6115 the controller's power domain, the USB30 GDSC, is PWRSTS_OFF_ON:
genpd switches it off for s2idle (and for runtime PM, once the glue's
power/control is "auto"), and the QSCRATCH registers come back at their
reset values. A peripheral then never sees VBUS again: on the F(x)tec
Pro1X the gadget stayed "not attached" after the first suspend, and
neither rebinding the gadget nor switching the USB role (the role switch
does not go through this glue) brought it back, only a reboot did. The
flattened glue (dwc3-qcom.c) sets the override again on every role
change and run/stop (pre_set_role, pre_run_stop) for the same reason;
downstream re-programs QSCRATCH after every power collapse.

Write both again in dwc3_qcom_resume(), after the clocks are back and
before the core resumes, from the mode probe and the notifiers keep.

"HS-PHY not in L2" at suspend: only a host's PHY is suspended there; for
a peripheral the core has powered its PHYs off (dwc3_core_exit()), so the
message (which the same suspend printed) is a debug message in that case.

Keeping the GDSC in retention (PWRSTS_RET_ON, as gcc-sm8250, sc7180,
sm6350 and sm6375 do for USB) would be the other way; it keeps the
controller's state but also its power domain on for every suspend.
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom-legacy.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom-legacy.c
index d3fad0fcfd..55542eec3a 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom-legacy.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-qcom-legacy.c
@@ -86,6 +86,7 @@ struct dwc3_qcom {
 	struct notifier_block	host_nb;
 
 	enum usb_dr_mode	mode;
+	bool			ignore_pipe_clk;
 	bool			is_suspended;
 	bool			pm_suspended;
 	struct icc_path		*icc_path_ddr;
@@ -423,18 +424,29 @@ static void dwc3_qcom_enable_interrupts(struct dwc3_qcom *qcom)
 		dwc3_qcom_enable_port_interrupts(&qcom->ports[i]);
 }
 
+static void dwc3_qcom_select_utmi_clk(struct dwc3_qcom *qcom);
+
 static int dwc3_qcom_suspend(struct dwc3_qcom *qcom, bool wakeup)
 {
+	bool host = dwc3_qcom_is_host(qcom);
 	u32 val;
 	int i, ret;
 
 	if (qcom->is_suspended)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * Only a host's PHY is suspended (L2) here; for a peripheral the core
+	 * has powered its PHYs off instead (dwc3_core_exit()).
+	 */
 	for (i = 0; i < qcom->num_ports; i++) {
 		val = readl(qcom->qscratch_base + pwr_evnt_irq_stat_reg[i]);
-		if (!(val & PWR_EVNT_LPM_IN_L2_MASK))
+		if (val & PWR_EVNT_LPM_IN_L2_MASK)
+			continue;
+		if (host)
 			dev_err(qcom->dev, "port-%d HS-PHY not in L2\n", i + 1);
+		else
+			dev_dbg(qcom->dev, "port-%d HS-PHY not in L2\n", i + 1);
 	}
 
 	for (i = qcom->num_clocks - 1; i >= 0; i--)
@@ -483,6 +495,17 @@ static int dwc3_qcom_resume(struct dwc3_qcom *qcom, bool wakeup)
 	if (ret)
 		dev_warn(qcom->dev, "failed to enable interconnect: %d\n", ret);
 
+	/*
+	 * The controller's power domain (the USB30 GDSC) may have been off,
+	 * and the QSCRATCH registers with it: what probe and the extcon
+	 * notifiers set is back at its reset value. Without the VBUS override
+	 * a peripheral never sees VBUS again and stays "not attached".
+	 */
+	if (qcom->ignore_pipe_clk)
+		dwc3_qcom_select_utmi_clk(qcom);
+	if (qcom->mode != USB_DR_MODE_HOST)
+		dwc3_qcom_vbus_override_enable(qcom, true);
+
 	/* Clear existing events from PHY related to L2 in/out */
 	for (i = 0; i < qcom->num_ports; i++) {
 		dwc3_qcom_setbits(qcom->qscratch_base,
@@ -790,6 +813,7 @@ static int dwc3_qcom_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	 */
 	ignore_pipe_clk = device_property_read_bool(dev,
 				"qcom,select-utmi-as-pipe-clk");
+	qcom->ignore_pipe_clk = ignore_pipe_clk;
 	if (ignore_pipe_clk)
 		dwc3_qcom_select_utmi_clk(qcom);
 
